WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
INTRO:
By now, I’m sure you’ve all heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:08.460
about the supply shortage. It’s all over the news&nbsp;
and many of you have likely experienced it first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.920
hand. It’s no secret that wait times and prices&nbsp;
for many everyday items have gone through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:19.680
roof. To make things worse, it looks like things&nbsp;
are only getting worse. Take Tesla for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.740
Wait time for the model 3 are 3 to 6 months.&nbsp;
Wait times for the model Y are 6 to 9 months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.620
Wait times for the model S are 3 to 6 months. Wait&nbsp;
times for the model X are 9 to 12 months. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:36.900
Cybertruck is nowhere to be found. Clearly,&nbsp;
Tesla has months of backlog for all of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:43.380
vehicles. Yet, their Q2 production numbers are&nbsp;
down nearly 50,000 in comparison to Q1. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.480
is despite opening two new gigafactories within&nbsp;
the past couple of months. According to Elon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.340
these factories are currently “gigantic money&nbsp;
furnaces” because they simply can’t get enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.780
parts to reach they scale that they need to.&nbsp;
It’s not just Tesla that’s facing these issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.760
either. Even Apple is worried that supply chain&nbsp;
constraints will affect their top line by several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.940
billion dollars. But, why is this still even an&nbsp;
issue? Our freight networks, trucking networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.620
airline networks, and communication networks&nbsp;
are all more advanced than ever. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.600
you would think that such an issue would be solved&nbsp;
within a matter of months. But, we’re two years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.700
into this problem and things are only getting&nbsp;
worse. So, what’s up with the supply shortage?

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:29.280
PANDEMIC:&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.920
Starting with the most obvious reason, we have the&nbsp;
pandemic. While much of the world has returned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.540
normal life, the effects of the pandemic are still&nbsp;
quite evident throughout the supply chain. First&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.160
of all, China is still regularly implementing&nbsp;
lockdowns in even some of their biggest cities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.960
like Shanghai. And given that a significant&nbsp;
portion of our everyday items are produced in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.920
China, it’s not surprising that these lockdowns&nbsp;
are taking a toll on the global marketplace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.440
It’s not just lockdowns that we have to worry&nbsp;
about either. Covid has brought with it a slew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:02.880
of new safety protocols from regular testing to&nbsp;
strict quarantine measures. And this has simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.980
added more friction when it comes to maximizing&nbsp;
production and logistics. But, even if lockdowns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:13.020
and covid protocols were left behind, we would&nbsp;
face issues regarding labor shortages. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:17.520
when the pandemic hit, many companies scaled back&nbsp;
production to prepare for potentially weak demand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.380
This meant shutting down factories and laying&nbsp;
off a significant number of employees. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:28.020
made sense at the time but demand has recovered&nbsp;
faster than anyone expected. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.220
in some industries, demand is higher than ever&nbsp;
because people have been excited to return to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:37.560
normal life. The problem, however, is that it&nbsp;
takes time to scale up production. While you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:43.380
shutdown a factory overnight, you can’t just turn&nbsp;
it back on overnight. A great example is Tesla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:48.840
Gigafactories. It generally takes years for these&nbsp;
behemoths to reach their strides, and even then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:54.720
they still have years worth of optimization left.&nbsp;
Also, it’s not just one or two factories that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.460
shutdown. Much of the supply chain as a whole&nbsp;
was shut down, so everyone is having to scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:04.380
back up all over again. To make things worse, it’s&nbsp;
been extremely difficult to find enough labor to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:09.480
run these factories and logistics networks. This&nbsp;
was already an issue before the pandemic started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.519
but the pandemic just made this problem&nbsp;
substantially worse. You see, many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:13.860
manufacturing employees that were furloughed&nbsp;
or laid off simply didn’t return. Instead they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:19.020
found new jobs that were more flexible and paid&nbsp;
better. By now, I’m sure you’ve all heard about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:24.780
the great resignation, and one of the hardest hit&nbsp;
industries in manufacturing. Right now, the US is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:30.300
short 3.5 million manufacturing workers and the&nbsp;
worst part is that 2 million of these positions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:35.160
are expected to go unfilled. This is definitely&nbsp;
not a good sign for supply chain issues easing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:40.800
anytime soon. And given that 82% of manufacturing&nbsp;
executives feel that labor shortages are one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.680
the key reasons that they’re struggling&nbsp;
to meet demand and increase productivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:49.440
this supply shortage could easily last for years&nbsp;
to come. It’s not just manufacturing workers that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.640
are missing either, we’re seeing shortages&nbsp;
in the logistics side of things as well. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.780
American Trucking Associations estimates that&nbsp;
the driver shortage has increased from roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:03.000
60,000 before the pandemic to 80,000 today. So,&nbsp;
not only has the pandemic reduced the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.960
people who are producing goods but also the&nbsp;
number of people who are delivering goods.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:13.320
INFLATION:
Aside from a major labor and scaling mess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:17.640
supply chains have also been challenged&nbsp;
by inflation. The price of raw materials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:21.960
have skyrocketed throughout the pandemic, and&nbsp;
while they have cooled down from their peaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:27.420
there’s still a ways to go. Take Aluminum for&nbsp;
example. Before the pandemic started, Aluminum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:32.940
cost roughly $1700 per tonne. At the start of&nbsp;
the pandemic, prices briefly cooled down to $1500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:39.360
before they exploded to nearly $4000 in February&nbsp;
of 2022. Since then, prices have come back down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:45.480
$2400, but that’s still 40% higher than where they&nbsp;
started. A similar story can be seen with Lumber.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:51.420
Before the pandemic, Lumber generally costed $3&nbsp;
to $400 per 1000 board feet. From there, Lumber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:58.560
crashed to $250 before rocketing to an eyewatering&nbsp;
$1600. Fortunately, we’ve cooled back down to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:04.560
little over $600, but that’s still over 60% higher&nbsp;
than where we started. This price volatility has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:08.460
made everyone within the supply chain extremely&nbsp;
cautious because they don’t want their businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:13.200
to become unprofitable. Here’s the thing,&nbsp;
let’s say you own the company that produces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.760
chassis for iPhones. You already committed to&nbsp;
making X number of chassis at a given price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:21.960
but since you made this commitment, the price of&nbsp;
aluminum has doubled which leaves your business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:28.260
unprofitable. At this point, you have two options.&nbsp;
You can either go to Apple and renegotiate a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:32.280
higher price due to rising material costs which&nbsp;
will likely take weeks if not months. Or you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:37.200
wait for aluminum prices to cool down before you&nbsp;
ramp up production which will also take weeks if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:42.060
not months. Meanwhile, if you’re about to form a&nbsp;
new manufacturing contract with let’s say Samsung,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:47.160
you’re probably going to quote them a higher price&nbsp;
to protect yourself from price fluctuations. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:51.180
when Samsung hears this high price, they’re gonna&nbsp;
be inclined to get more quotes before their lock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:57.000
in a manufacturing contract. And all of this just&nbsp;
slows down the process of meeting demand. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:01.260
keep in mind that most of these companies&nbsp;
aren’t just dealing with one supplier. Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:06.660
for example, has over 200 suppliers. So, Apple&nbsp;
would have to troubleshoot this same issue over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:12.360
200 times at the same time. I don’t think I need&nbsp;
to explain how this quickly turns into a supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:17.100
chain nightmare. To make things worse, Apple’s&nbsp;
issues won’t be solved once they get all of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:21.720
suppliers on board. Now, they have to worry about&nbsp;
actually transporting the finished product to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:27.600
customer. Fortunately, gas prices aren’t that&nbsp;
bad, wait nevermind crude oil is floating at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:33.780
over $100 per barrel. I don’t even think I need to&nbsp;
explain why this is a problem because most of you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:39.060
have likely dealt with obscene gas prices first&nbsp;
hand. Now, companies could just go ahead and raise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:44.280
prices like many of them have been doing, but with&nbsp;
recession fears being rampant, consumers are more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.840
price sensitive than ever. Last quarter,&nbsp;
Netflix lost nearly a million subscribers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:54.180
so there’s no doubt that consumers are cutting&nbsp;
back on expenses. So, the last thing companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:59.940
want to do is raise prices too fast and lose even&nbsp;
more customers. As a result, many companies have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:04.440
decided that its better to have customers wait as&nbsp;
they try to source cheaper materials than it is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:10.080
have customers leave because they raised priced.&nbsp;
This mindset is by no means helping the situation.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:15.660
RUSSIA-UKRAINE WAR:
The pandemic along with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:20.280
inflation were already massive challenges&nbsp;
to deal with, but now we also have to deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:25.740
with the Russia-Ukraine war. Neither Russia nor&nbsp;
Ukraine are massive manufacturing players per se,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.300
but this isn’t to say that they don’t have a&nbsp;
significant impact on the global supply chain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:36.240
Ukraine was one of the world’s top sources&nbsp;
for neon. Now, neon might seem irrelevant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.860
but it’s actually extremely relevant when&nbsp;
you consider that neon plays a crucial role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:45.960
in semiconductor manufacturing. You see, neon is&nbsp;
required to run the lasers that are used in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:50.460
chip making process. And given that 90% of the&nbsp;
neon that was used to produce US chips came from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:56.640
Ukraine, this is not a great sign. Neon wasn’t the&nbsp;
only item that came from Ukraine either. The war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:02.220
has also cut off significant supply of nickel,&nbsp;
aluminum, wheat, and sunflower oil. And all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:07.620
that is just Ukraine’s side of things. Russia is&nbsp;
third largest oil producer and second largest oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:13.020
exporter. Cutting of Russian oil is one of the&nbsp;
main reasons that gas prices have gone insane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:18.060
over the past few months. And given that these&nbsp;
sanctions likely wont be easing anytime soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:22.260
transportation networks are gonna have to get&nbsp;
used to higher fuel prices. Aside from oil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:27.480
Russia is also the world’s second largest natural&nbsp;
gas producer and the world’s largest natural gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:33.540
exporter. Given that the manufacturing industry&nbsp;
is heavily dependent on natural gas, they’re gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:37.740
have to get used to higher natural gas prices&nbsp;
for quite some time. Aside from raw materials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:43.200
the war has also dispruted major transportation&nbsp;
networks. Here’s the thing, Russia is literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:49.260
the biggest country in the world by far, so when&nbsp;
you cut off all cargo trains, planes, and ships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:54.300
from this region, it becomes a lot harder to&nbsp;
transport goods. In China, for example, over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:58.200
million containers became stuck in the logistics&nbsp;
channel when the war started. You see, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:01.740
containers were supposed to be transported to&nbsp;
Europe by a train that travels through Russia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:07.620
But, now all of them have to be transported&nbsp;
by sea. So, the Russia-Ukraine war is not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:12.120
taking a massive toll on raw materials but&nbsp;
also international transportation networks.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:19.560
THE SUPPLY SHORTAGE:
In the end, I think Matthew Rozsa says it best.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:24.120
The global supply chain is like a gigantic&nbsp;
spiderweb that was been created through decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:29.280
of producing, scaling, and delivering. And out&nbsp;
of nowhere, three giant baseballs have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:34.860
thrown at this spiderweb. The first baseball&nbsp;
is the pandemic. The lockdowns, layoffs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:39.780
and factory shutdowns brought on by Covid-19 have&nbsp;
been catastrophic. Scaling up production back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:44.340
full capacity is a time consuming endeavor and&nbsp;
this has been especially challenging due to labor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:49.800
shortages. The second baseball is inflation.&nbsp;
The price increases in raw materials, labor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:54.840
and fuel have led to higher expenses in every&nbsp;
step of the supply chain. Meanwhile, companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:59.220
are trying to minimize price hikes to maximize&nbsp;
customer retention. And this has simply led to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:04.020
companies putting supply chain issues on the back&nbsp;
burner instead of addressing them head on. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:09.060
finally, the third baseball is the Russian-Ukraine&nbsp;
war. Not only has the war cut off several key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:14.460
resources such as neon, natural gas, and oil, but&nbsp;
it has blocked off almost all cargo transportation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:18.420
through Russia. These three baseballs have&nbsp;
left three massive holes within the supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:23.580
chain spiderweb and this is not something that&nbsp;
can be fixed overnight. Given that many millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:27.660
of manufacturing jobs will remain vacant, the&nbsp;
clearest solution is to lean towards automation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:32.820
which will take years to develop. Similarly, it&nbsp;
took a year for inflation to ramp up to where we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:37.920
are right now. And even if this is the peak, it’ll&nbsp;
likely take another year for inflation to cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:44.040
back down. And as for the Russia situation, even&nbsp;
if the war ended today, it’ll take years if not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:49.440
over a decade to repair relations. So, our best&nbsp;
bet is to simply find new sources of natural gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:55.920
and oil and neon, but this will again take time.&nbsp;
So, you should probably buckle in because this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.920 --> 00:11:01.140
crazy ride is far from being over, but that’s just&nbsp;
what I think. When you do you think supply chain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:06.480
issues will ease? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;
drop a like if you hope supply chain issues ease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:11.040
sooner rather than later. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:14.520
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

